  realLife: [
    { tag: 'SELF-JUSTIFYING', tagZh: '自我辩护', verseRef: 'Proverbs 21:2',
      titleZh: '当你确信“我绝对没错”', titleEn: 'When you’re sure you’re totally right',
      bodyZh: '吵架、冲突、或做了某个决定后，我们几乎总能给自己找到一套说得通的理由——箴言 21:2 说，“人所行的，在自己眼中都看为正。”问题不是我们偶尔会错，而是我们既是当事人又是裁判，很难看清自己的动机。这节经文不是要我们陷入自我怀疑，而是邀请我们把心敞开，请那位“衡量人心”的神来照明：主啊，我可能看漏了什么？',
      bodyEn: 'After an argument, a conflict, or a decision, we can almost always assemble a story that makes us the reasonable one — Proverbs 21:2 says, “Every way of a man is right in his own eyes.” The problem isn’t that we’re occasionally wrong; it’s that we’re both defendant and judge and can’t see our own motives clearly. This verse isn’t a call to endless self-doubt but an invitation to open the heart and let the God who “weighs hearts” shine light in: Lord, what am I not seeing?' },
    { tag: 'MONEY', tagZh: '金钱', verseRef: 'Proverbs 21:5',
      titleZh: '“快钱”与踏实积累', titleEn: '“Fast money” versus steady building',
      bodyEn: 'Proverbs 21:5 sets diligent planning against haste: the diligent move toward profit, the hasty rush toward want. It’s a timely word in an age of get-rich-quick schemes, impulse spending, and “this deal closes tonight.” The verse doesn’t praise timidity — it praises planning. Wealth that lasts is usually built the slow, boring, honest way; the gain that promises to skip all that (21:6) tends to vanish like vapor. Before the next fast opportunity, ask: is this diligence, or just haste dressed up as opportunity?',
      bodyZh: '箴言 21:5 把“殷勤筹划”和“急躁贪快”对立起来：殷勤的走向丰裕，急躁的奔向缺乏。在这个充满“一夜暴富”“限时秒杀”“今晚不买就没了”的时代，这句话格外及时。它不是在赞美胆小，而是在赞美有计划。能长久的财富，通常是靠慢的、枯燥的、诚实的方式积累起来的；而那些承诺让你跳过这一切的“机会”（21:6），往往像浮云一样吹散。下次面对“快钱”时，先问：这是殷勤，还是被包装成机会的急躁？' },
    { tag: 'GENEROSITY', tagZh: '慷慨', verseRef: 'Proverbs 21:13',
      titleZh: '对求助声充耳不闻', titleEn: 'Tuning out the cry for help',
      bodyZh: '箴言 21:13 说得很严肃：塞耳不听穷人哀求的，将来自己呼求也不蒙应允。我们很容易在忙碌、疲惫、或自我保护中，慢慢学会“看不见”身边的需要。这节经文提醒我们，冷漠不是没有立场——它本身就是一种选择，而且会反过来塑造我们。慷慨不一定是给出多大一笔钱，有时只是愿意停下来，真正地听见另一个人的处境。',
      bodyEn: 'Proverbs 21:13 is sobering: whoever shuts his ears to the cry of the poor will himself cry out and not be heard. In busyness, exhaustion, or self-protection, we can slowly train ourselves not to “see” the need around us. This verse reminds us that indifference is not the absence of a position — it is itself a choice, and it shapes us in return. Generosity isn’t always a large gift; sometimes it’s simply the willingness to stop and truly hear another person’s situation.' },
    { tag: 'PLANNING', tagZh: '筹划', verseRef: 'Proverbs 21:31',
      titleZh: '尽力预备，把结果交托', titleEn: 'Prepare fully, then entrust the result',
      bodyZh: '“马是为打仗之日预备的，得胜乃在乎耶和华”（21:31）。这节经文精准地平衡了两种极端：一种是什么都不做，美其名曰“交托神”；另一种是拼命筹划，仿佛结果全靠自己。圣经说：去预备你的马——尽你的责任，做该做的功课；然后把胜负交给神。考试、面试、手术、重大决定前，我们都可以这样祷告：主啊，我已尽力预备，如今把结果交在你手中。',
      bodyEn: 'The horse is prepared for the day of battle, but victory is with the LORD (21:31). This verse holds two extremes in balance: doing nothing and calling it “trusting God,” versus planning frantically as if the result were entirely up to us. Scripture says: prepare your horse — do your part, put in the work — and then hand the outcome to God. Before an exam, an interview, a surgery, a big decision, we can pray: Lord, I’ve prepared as best I can; now I place the result in your hands.' },
  ],

